<p>Syria opened its first public trial of former officials linked to the government of ousted President Bashar al-Assad in Damascus on April 26. Former Daraa security chief Atef Najib appeared in court, while Bashar al-Assad and his brother Maher are to be tried in absentia. The hearing marks an early step by Syria’s new authorities, who have repeatedly said they want accountability for abuses committed during the Assad era and have cast the case as part of a broader transitional justice process in a country still recovering from years of conflict. The case carries special weight because Najib is closely tied to events in Daraa, where the 2011 detention and abuse of boys accused of anti-government graffiti helped ignite the uprising against Assad. For many Syrians and rights advocates, the opening of a public proceeding is a visible sign that long-delayed efforts to document abuses and pursue justice are beginning to move through Syria’s own courts, even as major questions remain about due process, accountability for absent defendants, and the scale of crimes still to be addressed.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/t-XbEW6FK8IO1OFxYnhGF43vZc0cCVHW-0JZ0E5l_uMbc6O3efJ1M0Eo9HkaWHN3L2o42EEw5QCKiCi0Mot94mMDliy3Tq2AiIOlpsieKYRKwqe-_54DcowFWnrON_uUoXHOUBgdOmHl-nz5Lr_qNG5sizDqQT1GeOiULsKT6dRylF-6lFvpPblwlKUhWtmRMX872Q6t8U2BMOv9f99Sskzt7v4VMNsuPyfI2AOMIUhcx1XcEMqui6w2AA' alt='People gather during the first trial session of Atef Najib in Damascus, Syria, April 26, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Who appeared: Atef Najib, a relative of Bashar al-Assad and former head of security in Daraa Governorate, was the only defendant physically present in the dock and appeared in handcuffs during the opening session.</li><li>Absent defendants: Bashar al-Assad and Maher al-Assad are set to face proceedings in absentia rather than appear before the Damascus court.</li><li>Judge’s framing: Judge Fakhr al-Din al-Aryan opened the session by calling it the beginning of Syria’s first transitional justice trials, underscoring the authorities’ effort to connect the case to wider postwar accountability.</li><li>Accountability push: Activists and international observers say credible transitional justice will be important to rebuilding trust in Syria after years of conflict and reported atrocities under Assad’s rule.</li></ul><blockquote>Today we begin the first trials of transitional justice in Syria - Judge Fakhr al-Din al-Aryan</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Syrian new authorities: The country’s new leadership has repeatedly promised justice and accountability for abuses committed during the Assad era, presenting the trial as an opening step in a wider process of national reckoning. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/26/syrian-court-conducts-first-hearing-in-trial-of-bashar-al-assad_6752859_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Activists: Activists have stressed that transitional justice must be central to Syria’s recovery, signaling that public trials need to lead to broader accountability rather than remain symbolic. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/26/syrian-court-conducts-first-hearing-in-trial-of-bashar-al-assad_6752859_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Syrian court: The presiding judge explicitly cast the proceedings as the start of transitional justice in Syria, suggesting the court sees the case as more than a single prosecution. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/syrian-court-begins-proceedings-against-assad-and-allies'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/syrias-public-trial-assad-era-officials-opens-damascus-132394512'>Syria’s first public trial of Assad-era officials opens in Damascus</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/26/syrian-court-conducts-first-hearing-in-trial-of-bashar-al-assad_6752859_4.html'>Syrian court conducts first 